<h2>Act vs. Rule Utilitarianism</h2>
<p><em>Putting the two utilitarian frameworks to work on a real case</em></p>
<p>Utilitarianism comes in two main versions, and they often give different answers. An act utilitarian asks of each individual action: which option produces the most happiness here? A rule utilitarian asks instead: which general rule, if generally followed, would produce the most happiness? In most ordinary cases the two converge, but in some cases they diverge sharply. This reflection asks you to find one of those cases and work through it.</p>
<div class="callout callout--concept"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#128161;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>The prompt</strong></p>
<p><strong>Step one: Pick a specific moral case where breaking a rule seems to produce more happiness than following it.</strong> The case should be concrete enough to analyze, with real stakes, real consequences, and at least some plausibility on both sides. You can draw from your own life, from current events, or from a constructed scenario. Examples to consider: lying to a sick relative to spare them bad news, breaking a confidence to prevent harm, helping someone cheat on an exam if they really need to pass, downloading paid material you cannot afford, jumping a line in a genuine emergency. Find your own; do not just take one of these.</p>
<p><strong>Step two: Apply both frameworks step by step.</strong></p>
<ol>
<li>State the case clearly: what is the action, who is affected, what are the relevant alternatives?</li>
<li>Apply the act utilitarian framework. What does the calculation actually look like in this case? What pleasures and pains are at stake, for whom, and how do they sum? Be specific. "It produces more good overall" is not enough; you have to show the work.</li>
<li>Apply the rule utilitarian framework. What general rule is at stake? What would happen if that rule were generally followed, or generally broken? Compare the long-run consequences of the rule versus its absence, not just the consequences of this one case.</li>
<li>Defend a position. Which framework gives the better answer for your case, and why? Notice that this is not just a matter of which framework you prefer in general; it is a matter of which framework gets the right answer in <em>this case</em>, and why.</li>
<li>Address one challenge to your preferred framework. The most natural objection to act utilitarianism is that it permits actions almost everyone considers wrong (framing the innocent, breaking promises whenever convenient). The most natural objection to rule utilitarianism is that it can require us to follow a rule even when we know it produces worse outcomes in the particular case. Acknowledge one such challenge and explain how you would respond.</li>
</ol>
</div></div>
<div class="callout callout--note"><span class="callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">&#8505;&#65039;</span><div class="callout__content">
<p><strong>A common failure mode</strong></p>
<p>The most common mistake in utilitarian reasoning is hand-waving the calculation. Saying "act utilitarianism would weigh the consequences and conclude that..." without actually doing any weighing is not yet an analysis. Do the math, at the level a real moral reasoner would do it. Name the people affected. Name the pleasures and pains in play. Take seriously the long-run effects that rule utilitarianism is supposed to track. If you find yourself describing the case at a level of generality where the conclusion is obvious in advance, your case is probably too generic to teach you anything. Make it specific.</p>
</div></div>
